In C, a program can be terminated several ways:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    return 0;   // normal termination; status 0 conventionally means success
}
```

Equivalent explicit form:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);
}
```

Common options:

- `return 0;` from `main` — normal, preferred in simple programs.
- `exit(status);` — normal termination from any function; runs functions registered with `atexit()` and flushes/closes standard I/O streams.
- `_Exit(status);` — immediate process termination; does not run `atexit()` handlers or flush stdio buffers.
- `abort();` — abnormal termination, usually producing `SIGABRT` and potentially a core dump.

Example error termination:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    fprintf(stderr, "Fatal error\n");
    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
}
```

Use `EXIT_SUCCESS` and `EXIT_FAILURE` from `<stdlib.h>` when portability and clarity matter.
